## The Bug

The goroutines capture the **loop variable `i` by reference** (closure over a shared variable), not by value. By the time any goroutine actually runs, the `for` loop has already completed and `i` has reached its final value of `5`.

In Go 1.21 (and earlier), the loop variable `i` is a single variable reused across all iterations. All five goroutines read the same `i`, which is `5` at the time they execute.

> **Note:** Go 1.22 changed the loop variable semantics so that each iteration gets its own variable, which would make your original code work. But since you're on 1.21, you need an explicit fix.

## The Fix

Pass `i` as an argument to the anonymous function. This creates a new variable per goroutine that captures the value at the moment the goroutine is spawned:

```go
go func(i int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)
}(i)
```

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(i int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(i)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

